1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,250
Hi. Great to see you!

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:10,550
I'm Jody, and I'll be looking after both of you for the first month you're working here at the Amersham Theatre.

3
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:14,240
I'll tell you something about the theatre now

4
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:16,940
then take you to meet two of the other staff.

5
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:22,450
It's an old building, and it's been modernised several times.

6
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:28,450
In fact, as you can see, we're carrying out a major refurbishment at the moment.

7
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:30,817
The interior has just been repainted,

8
00:00:30,817 --> 00:00:37,130
and we're about to start on the exterior of the building that'll be a big job.

9
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:43,110
The work's running over budget, so we've had to postpone installing an elevator.

10
00:00:43,110 --> 00:00:46,420
I hope you're happy running up and down stairs!

11
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:48,573
When the theatre was built,

12
00:00:48,573 --> 00:00:52,500
people were generally slimmer and shorter than now

13
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:55,030
and the seats were very close together.

14
00:00:55,030 --> 00:00:59,500
We've replaced them with larger seats, with more legroom.

15
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:02,580
This means fewer seats in total

16
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,812
but we've taken the opportunity to install seats that can easily be moved,

17
00:01:07,812 --> 00:01:10,000
to create different acting spaces.

18
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,460
We've also turned a few storerooms over to other purposes

19
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:16,520
like using them for meetings.

20
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:21,800
We try hard to involve members of the public in the theatre.

21
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:24,800
One way is by organising backstage tours,

22
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:30,800
so people can be shown round the building and learn how a theatre operates.

23
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:33,250
These are proving very popular.

24
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:39,968
What we're finding is that people want to have lunch or a cup of coffee while they're here,

25
00:01:39,968 --> 00:01:44,820
so we're looking into the possibility of opening a cafe in due course.

26
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:50,240
We have a bookshop, which specialises in books about drama

27
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:52,500
and that attracts plenty of customers.

28
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:57,540
Then there are two large rooms that will be decorated next month,

29
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:59,820
and they'll be available for hire

30
00:02:00,180 --> 00:02:03,420
for conferences and private functions, such as parties.

31
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:08,270
We're also considering hiring out costumes to amateur drama clubs.

32
00:02:08,270 --> 00:02:13,140
Now I want to tell you about our workshops.

33
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:18,400
We recently started a programme of workshops that anyone can join.

34
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:21,634
Eventually we intend to run courses in acting,

35
00:02:21,634 --> 00:02:26,890
but we're waiting until we've got the right people in place as trainers.

36
00:02:26,890 --> 00:02:30,810
That's proving more difficult than we'd expected!

37
00:02:30,810 --> 00:02:36,924
There's a big demand to learn about the technical side of putting on a production,

38
00:02:36,924 --> 00:02:41,000
and our lighting workshop has already started, with great success.

39
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,680
We're going to start one on sound next month.

40
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:48,663
A number of people have enquired about workshops on make-up,

41
00:02:48,663 --> 00:02:51,850
and that's something we're considering for the future.

42
00:02:51,850 --> 00:02:56,840
A surprise success is the workshop on making puppets

43
00:02:57,400 --> 00:02:58,024
we happen to have someone working here

44
00:02:58,024 --> 00:03:03,040
who does it as a hobby, and she offered to run a workshop.

45
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:07,080
It was so popular we've now running them every month!

46
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:13,000
Now, a word about the layout of the building.

47
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:19,500
The auditorium, stage and dressing rooms for the actors are all below ground level.

48
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:25,000
Here on the ground floor we have most of the rooms that the public doesn't see.

49
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,700
The majority are internal, so they have windows in the roof to light them.

50
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:32,798
Standing here in the foyer,

51
00:03:32,798 --> 00:03:37,480
you're probably wondering why the box office isn't here

52
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:39,930
where the public would expect to find it.

53
00:03:39,930 --> 00:03:45,967
Well, you might have noticed it on your way in although it's part of this building,

54
00:03:45,967 --> 00:03:49,740
it's next door with a separate entrance from the road.

55
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:52,800
For the theatre manager's office

56
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:55,790
you go across the foyer and through the double doors,

57
00:03:55,790 --> 00:04:03,050
turn right, and it's the room at the end of the corridor, with the door on the left.

58
00:04:03,050 --> 00:04:09,380
The lighting box is where the computerised stage lighting is operated

59
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:11,830
and it's at the back of the building.

60
00:04:11,830 --> 00:04:16,260
When you're through the double doors, turn left

61
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:21,180
turn right at the water cooler and right again at the end.

62
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:24,600
It's the second room along that corridor.

63
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:31,000
The lighting box has a window into the auditorium, which of course is below us.

64
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,281
The artistic director's office is through the double doors,

65
00:04:35,281 --> 00:04:41,940
turn right, and it's the first room you come to on the right-hand side.

66
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:44,960
And finally, for the moment

67
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:49,000
the room where I'll take you next - the relaxation room.

68
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,200
So if you'd like to come with me...
